<Base title="Rhodes"
  eyebrow="Santou Isaac Codron, 1913–1997"
  dek="Hedviga's second husband was born on Rhodes in 1913, into a Sephardic community that was almost entirely destroyed in 1944 — and he died in South Africa in 1997. How he came to be alive to marry her is the most interesting unanswered question in this archive.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The tree records him plainly and briefly:
    <strong>Santou (Shemtov) Isaac Codron</strong>, born <strong>13 September 1913 at
    Rhodes</strong>, died <strong>16 April 1997 in South Africa</strong> of cardio-respiratory
    arrest, married to <a href={u("/hedviga/")}>Hedviga</a> about <strong>1973</strong>. That is
    every fact held on him. Each one opens onto a large history.</p>
    <p><strong>Shemtov</strong> — <em>shem tov</em>, “good name” — is a Sephardic given name, and
    <strong>Codron</strong> is a known Rhodes Jewish surname. He was, on the face of the record, a
    <em>Rhodesli</em>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">23 July 1944</div>
    <h2>What happened to the community he was born into</h2>
    <p>Rhodes held a Sephardic Jewish community that had been there since the fifteenth century,
    speaking <strong>Judeo-Spanish</strong> — the language their ancestors carried out of Spain in
    1492. In the quarter called <em>La Djuderia</em> it was still, in 1944, a living world.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">On <strong>20 July 1944</strong> the SS ordered the island's Jews to
    assemble for a document check. <strong>1,615 people from Rhodes</strong>, together with
    <strong>94 from Kos</strong>, were packed into the cargo holds of livestock ships, taken to the
    Greek mainland, and put on a train. They arrived at Auschwitz on <strong>16 August 1944</strong>
    after what is generally called <strong>the longest deportation journey of the
    Holocaust</strong>. About 1,200 were murdered on arrival. <strong>Roughly 150
    survived.</strong></p>
    <div class="note">Santou Codron was <strong>thirty</strong> in July 1944. If he was on Rhodes
    that month, he was almost certainly on that transport — and the odds of walking out of it were
    about one in eleven.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The likelier explanation, and why</h2>
    <p class="lede">He probably was not there — because the Rhodes Jews had been leaving for
    <strong>southern Africa</strong> for fifty years, and that is exactly where he turns up.</p>
    <p>Rhodesli men began going to Africa for work at the end of the nineteenth century;
    <strong>Mousa and Solomon Benatar</strong> settled in Southern Rhodesia in
    <strong>1895</strong>. Emigration accelerated through the 1920s and 1930s, to the
    <strong>Belgian Congo</strong>, <strong>Southern Rhodesia</strong> and
    <strong>South Africa</strong> — for economic reasons first, and from
    <strong>1938</strong> because of the Italian racial laws, Rhodes then being Italian territory.
    Sephardim from Rhodes settled in <strong>Salisbury</strong>, today's Harare, and founded a
    synagogue there in <strong>1932</strong>. In all, close to
    <strong>two thousand Jews from Rhodes</strong> lived in the Congo and Southern Rhodesia between
    the 1920s and the 1960s.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Now set Hedviga's own movements beside that.
    <strong>She came to South Africa from Zimbabwe — that is, from Rhodesia — in April 1975</strong>,
    and she is recorded as marrying Codron around <strong>1973</strong>, which places the marriage
    in the <em>Rhodesian</em> years.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>So the meeting has an obvious setting.</strong> A Croatian woman from
    Senj and a Sephardic Jew from Rhodes, both a long way from home, in the small European society
    of late-colonial Rhodesia — a community he most likely belonged to because his family had been
    part of that specific migration for decades before the war.
    <strong>This is an inference, not a document.</strong></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 14 September 2026 · Find a Grave</div>
    <h2>The question this page asked itself, answered — and it is not a comfortable answer</h2>
    <p class="lede">The table below has said since it was written that <em>who among the Codrons was
    deported in July 1944 has not been checked here</em>. It has now been checked — not against Yad
    Vashem, which remains the right register, but against the index of gravestones. It took an
    afternoon and it should have been done long ago.</p>

    <div class="qa">
      <h3>Fourteen Codron memorials at Auschwitz. Eight of them share one date.</h3>
      <p>That date is <strong>16 August 1944</strong> — the day this page already names as the
      arrival of the Rhodes transport, after the longest deportation journey of the Holocaust.</p>
      <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1rem">
        <thead><tr><th>Born</th><th>Name</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">1873</td><td>Caden Hasson Codron</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">20 Nov 1873</td><td>Ruben Bension Codron</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">3 Aug 1900</td><td>Leone Codron</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">21 Mar 1902</td><td>Hitzkia Codron</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">20 Jan 1906</td><td>Nissim Codron</td><td class="mono">1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">21 Dec 1906</td><td>Clara Simsolo Codron</td><td class="mono">1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">11 Feb 1907</td><td>Amelia Hasson Codron</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">9 Dec 1911</td><td>Regina Capelluto Codron</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">20 Apr 1925</td><td>Maurizio Mosè Codron</td><td class="mono">1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">11 Apr 1927</td><td>Lina Codron</td><td class="mono">1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">26 Jun 1928</td><td>Maria Codron</td><td class="mono">1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">1 Sep 1929</td><td><strong>Elsa Codron</strong> — fourteen</td><td class="mono">1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">24 Jul 1933</td><td><strong>Alessandro Codron</strong> — eleven</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">28 Apr 1939</td><td><strong>Rachele Codron</strong> — five</td><td class="mono">16 Aug 1944</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem">The married surnames carried into the family are
      <strong>Hasson</strong>, <strong>Simsolo</strong> and <strong>Capelluto</strong> — all of them
      Rhodes Sephardic names. <strong>Santou Codron was thirty-one when this happened, and he was
      alive in 1997 to be buried in South Africa.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The cemetery on the island — 1768 to 1937</h2>
    <p class="lede">The same index holds about <strong>twenty Codron graves in the Jewish Cemetery
    of the Island of Rhodes</strong>, with the row numbers, running from <strong>1768</strong> to
    <strong>1937</strong> — that is, from before the American revolution to seven years before the
    community was destroyed.</p>
    <p><strong>Yaacov Codron, 1768</strong> is the earliest. Then Sara De Salomon-Jeuda (1871),
    David De Abraham (1878), Dona De Joseph-Franco (1892), Rica De Haim (1892), Rosa De Abraham
    (1899), Lunar De Hizkia (1901), Raffael (1902), Isaac De Joseph and Miriam De Nissim (1904),
    Rebecca <em>Mujer de</em> Samuel-Israel (1910), Isaac De Abraham (1918), Risula and Samuel De
    Matalbria (1920), Abraham De Nissim (1923), Behora Simha David (1926), Paligoria De
    Behor-Menasce and Rachel De Reuben (1932), Risula De Isaac (1937), and
    <strong>Caden De Abraham Signoru Franco Codron, 1852–1941</strong>.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Look at how they are named.</strong> <em>De Abraham</em>,
    <em>De Nissim</em>, <em>De Joseph</em>, <em>De Haim</em>, <em>De Reuben</em> — son or daughter
    <em>of</em>. <em>Mujer de</em> — wife of. This archive has spent weeks on the Croatian
    equivalent: <em>«Georgius Jellicich, <strong>Antonii</strong>»</em> in the Selce death register,
    the patronymic that distinguishes one man of a common name from another.
    <strong>Two communities that never met, a thousand miles apart, solving the same problem the
    same way.</strong></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And a Codron family in Cape Town</h2>
    <p class="lede">Six Codrons lie in the <strong>Pinelands Jewish cemeteries at Cape Town</strong>
    — which is the other end of the migration this page argues he took.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Died</th><th>Name</th><th>Cemetery</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">17 Jun 1972</td><td>Leon Codron</td><td class="dek">Pinelands 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">26 May 1974</td><td>Jack Codron</td><td class="dek">Pinelands 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">13 Jun 1985</td><td>Albert Codron</td><td class="dek">Pinelands 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">15 Mar 1989</td><td>Raie Codron</td><td class="dek">Pinelands 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">14 Oct 1997</td><td><strong>Guditha Codron</strong></td><td class="dek">Pinelands 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">16 Aug 2000</td><td><strong>Isaac Nissim Codron</strong></td><td class="dek">Pinelands 2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Two of those are worth staring at.</strong>
    <strong>Guditha Codron died on 14 October 1997 — six months after Santou</strong>, in the same
    country. And <strong>Isaac Nissim Codron</strong> carries both of the names that surround him:
    Santou's own full name is <strong>Santou <em>Isaac</em> Codron</strong>, and <em>Nissim</em> is
    a Codron name on Rhodes three times over in the list above.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.8rem">Codrons also appear at the <strong>Stockholm Jewish Memorial</strong>
    — a survivors' memorial — at the <strong>Melbourne Chevra Kadisha</strong>, and at
    <strong>Home of Peace Memorial Park in Los Angeles</strong>, one of them in a plot marked
    <em>Western Jewish Institute</em>. Rhodes, Auschwitz, Cape Town, Stockholm, Melbourne, Los
    Angeles: the shape of what happened to one island's families.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>None of this is joined to Santou by a document.</strong> It is the
    same surname, the same island and the same community, and that is all it is. What it does is
    answer the question this page has been asking itself: <strong>the Codrons of Rhodes were
    deported, and fourteen of them are memorialised at Auschwitz.</strong> Whether he lost his own
    parents and brothers and sisters there, or whether his branch had left for Africa in the 1920s
    as this page argues, is still not known — <strong>and the two possibilities are no longer
    equally comfortable.</strong> The Auschwitz entries are <em>memorials, not graves</em>, and the
    index is compiled by volunteers, not an archive. <a href={u("/searched/")}>The search register
    →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this archive does and does not claim</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Documented</th><td>Born 13 September 1913, Rhodes. Died 16 April 1997, South Africa,
        of cardio-respiratory arrest. Married Hedviga Blažević <em>c.</em> 1973.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Inferred</th><td>That he was a Rhodes Sephardi — from the given name
        <em>Shemtov</em>, the surname <em>Codron</em> and the birthplace together.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Inferred</th><td>That he most likely reached southern Africa <strong>before</strong>
        the war, as part of the documented Rhodes-to-Rhodesia migration — because he was alive in
        1973 and because that route was well travelled.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">Not claimed</th><td><strong>That he survived Auschwitz.</strong> Nothing
        in this record says he was deported, and treating him as a survivor because he was born in
        the right place at the right time would be an invention. It would also be a serious one.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">Not claimed</th><td>That his family escaped — or that it did not.
        <strong>Checked, 14 September 2026:</strong> fourteen Codrons are memorialised at Auschwitz
        and eight of them share the date 16 August 1944. <strong>Not one of them is yet joined to
        him by a document.</strong> Yad Vashem and the Rhodes Jewish Museum registers remain the
        place to settle it.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>What would settle it.</strong> South African
    immigration and naturalisation papers would give his arrival year and last residence outright.
    The <strong>Rhodes Jewish Museum</strong> and <strong>Yad Vashem</strong> hold community and
    deportation registers in which a Codron born 13 September 1913 would either appear or be
    absent — and either answer is worth having.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
